    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "That's one small barre across three strings, or three fingers side by side — about as little as a fretted grip can ask of you. And it isn't new: play an open A chord, "
        },
        { "text": "x 0 2 2 2 0", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ", and stop at its middle three strings — 4, 3, and 2, all sitting at fret 2. That's "
        },
        { "text": "E3 A3 C#4", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " — an A major triad, second inversion, on this exact set. Chapter 1 used open A's "
        },
        { "text": "top", "marks": ["italic"] },
        {
          "text": " three strings; this is a different reading of the same chord, and you've had it under your fingers this whole time."
        }
      ]
    },
